      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on a ProForm Carbon TL?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can jog gently on it, and most people will find that fine. Sustained running is outside its design envelope — a 55-inch deck constrains stride as pace rises, and you end up shortening your stride or drifting toward the rear roller. The 10 mph top speed is the edge of the range rather than a statement about what the machine is for. For running, deck length is where the money should go.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the ProForm Carbon TL work without iFit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. There is a manual mode — the belt runs, the incline works and the display shows time, distance and speed with no account required. What you lose is most of the console's purpose, since the display is compact and the onboard programme selection is thin compared with brands that assume you will never subscribe. It works indefinitely unsubscribed; it just is not what the machine was designed around.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the ProForm Carbon TL deck size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ProForm publishes 20 by 55 inches. That is a walking and light-jogging specification: fine at any walking speed, fine for a gentle jog for most people, and increasingly constrained as pace rises. Sixty inches is the threshold at which an average-height adult stops noticing the deck while running, so this sits comfortably below it — and further below for taller users.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">ProForm Carbon TL vs Horizon T101 — which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are close on hardware and differ on terms. The T101 comes with no subscription in the picture and a substantially longer frame and motor warranty; the Carbon TL is an inexpensive route onto iFit and folds into a small footprint. For a walker with no interest in a platform, the Horizon is frequently the better buy. For someone who wants iFit cheaply, the ProForm makes more sense.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Carbon TL warranty any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is short next to Sole and Horizon at similar money, both of which commonly offer lifetime frame and motor cover at this price. A manufacturer's warranty is the clearest statement it makes about expected service life, so a shorter motor term is worth reading as exactly that. Terms have varied between model years, so confirm the current ones against the specific unit before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ProForm a reliable brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ProForm sits in the same corporate family as NordicTrack, which means a real parts and service network behind the machine — spares availability, a support operation and technicians who have seen it before. That is a genuine advantage over the anonymous brands that fill this price band, where a failed controller board simply ends the treadmill because nothing is available for it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Carbon TL</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Nearly every complaint about this machine comes from the first mistake, and it is made before the box is opened.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it as a running treadmill because the price is right</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 55-inch deck is the design envelope, not conservative marketing, and the 10 mph top speed is the edge of the range rather than an invitation. Running on it habitually works the motor harder than it was built for and shortens the machine’s life. If running is the plan, put the money into deck length instead, even if that means waiting a few months.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the machine is useless without a subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is not — there is a manual mode, and the belt and incline work permanently without an account. Plenty of people avoid this machine believing they would be locked out, and plenty of others subscribe unnecessarily. Decide whether you want iFit on its merits, add its cost to the purchase price if you do, and know that the hardware works either way.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the warranty gap against the direct competition</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sole and Horizon commonly offer lifetime frame and motor cover at similar money and ProForm does not match it. That is the manufacturer’s own statement about expected service life, and it is the strongest single argument for cross-shopping the Horizon T101 before ordering. Confirm current terms too, because they have changed between model years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping lubrication on a machine with a short warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Rising belt-to-deck friction is what kills budget treadmills, and it does so gradually by making the motor work harder every month. On a machine whose motor cover runs out early, that maintenance is the difference between five years and two. Ten minutes every few months, plus a belt-centring check, costs almost nothing and is never an economy to skip.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

